<h2>What the CAW Cell stage actually is</h2>
<p>The Cell performs two functions at once. It is a conciliation forum: joint sittings are fixed, both families are heard, and a substantial share of matrimonial complaints end here in settlement — reconciliation, agreed separation, or terms later formalised as mutual-consent divorce. It is also a filter: the inquiry officer records both versions, collects documents, and reports whether the complaint discloses a cognizable offence. The stage is administrative, not judicial; no one is an "accused" yet. But everything placed before the Cell — attendance, documents, conduct — becomes part of the narrative that an FIR, if registered, will inherit.</p>

<h2>Reading the escalation signals</h2>
<div class="flow">
<div class="fstep"><strong>Notice issued</strong><p>A complaint exists; conciliation is beginning. Apprehension of arrest is usually weak at this point.</p></div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>Sittings fail</strong><p>Positions harden; the complainant declines settlement or seeks prosecution. The inquiry moves toward a recommendation.</p></div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>Recommendation / closure</strong><p>The Cell either closes the complaint or recommends registration of an FIR. The latter converts apprehension from speculative to specific.</p></div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>FIR registered</strong><p>Arrest powers arise, subject to the statutory restraint applicable to offences punishable up to seven years — notice of appearance under Section 35(3) BNSS rather than automatic arrest.</p></div>
</div>
<p>Anticipatory bail strategy maps onto this ladder. Filing at the first rung risks dismissal as premature and announces alarm; waiting past the last rung risks an arrest before the application is heard. The conventional window opens when conciliation has visibly failed and closes when the FIR is registered — though a registered FIR does not bar anticipatory bail, it changes the hearing from apprehension to allegation.</p>

<h2>The statutory restraint on arrest — and why AB still matters</h2>
<p>Offences like Section 85 BNS carry a maximum of three years, which places them within the discipline that requires police to justify arrest as necessary and, ordinarily, to proceed by notice of appearance. That discipline has genuinely reduced automatic arrests in matrimonial cases. It has not made anticipatory bail redundant: notices can convert into arrest on alleged non-cooperation; allied provisions with higher punishments are often added; and for many clients the certainty of judicially-ordered protection, with conditions they can live with, is worth more than a statutory presumption applied at a police station. An anticipatory bail order also stabilises the settlement talks themselves — negotiations conducted under threat of arrest are rarely even-handed.</p>

<h2>Preparing the application</h2>
<p>A CAW-stage anticipatory bail application is won on documents. Annex the complaint or notice, proof of attendance at every sitting, the family\'s separate-residence evidence for implicated relatives, and the client\'s roots in the community — employment, property, prior clean record. Offer the standard conditions proactively: cooperation with the investigation, appearance on notice, no contact with the complainant except through counsel or mediation, and no travel without leave where that is realistic. Candour matters: suppression of a parallel proceeding or an earlier failed application is the fastest route to dismissal. And keep the matrimonial strategy aligned — the criminal and family proceedings are two faces of one dispute, and courts notice when the positions taken in each do not match.</p>

<div class="note">
<p>This is a general explainer on Delhi practice and the Bharatiya Nagarik Suraksha Sanhita, 2023; it is not legal advice. The CAW Cell\'s procedures are administrative and evolve; the decision whether and when to seek anticipatory bail turns on the specific complaint and its trajectory.</p>
</div>
